    meditation: `The first treatise on friendship in the Western tradition was written by a lawyer. Cicero composed De Amicitia in 44 BC, the year of Caesar's assassination, in a republic visibly dying around him. He set the dialogue in the mouth of Laelius, the great jurist and friend of Scipio Africanus the Younger, just after Scipio's death. The frame is a working lawyer asking an old advocate, between cases, how to think about the friend he has just lost. The book is short. You can read it in an evening. It has been read for two thousand years because nothing better on the subject has been written outside the Gospels.

Cicero defines friendship, in a sentence the Latin Mass tradition has half-remembered ever since, as omnium divinarum humanarumque rerum cum benevolentia et caritate consensio — an accord in all things, divine and human, joined with goodwill and affection. The definition is wider than ours. We use the word friend now for the man we play tennis with on Saturday and for the woman we have known since law school and would call from the side of a highway at 2 AM; the same word does both jobs, and it has been hollowed out by being asked to do so much. Cicero is reserving the word. A friend is one with whom you agree about what matters. The agreement is not on opinions; it is on the divinarum humanarumque rerum, the things divine and human — on what the good is, on what God is owed, on what a human life is for. Without that, you have an alliance, a colleagueship, a useful acquaintance. You do not have a friend.

A lawyer's life is full of useful acquaintances. The bar association mixer; the firm dinner where everyone is on a phone; the lunches kept on calendar because someone might refer a case someday. None of it is contemptible. Most of it is necessary. But it is not friendship in Cicero's sense, and you would die of loneliness if it were all you had. The professional life specializes in producing high volumes of cordial transaction. The danger is not that you will hate the people you work with. The danger is that you will mistake cordiality for friendship and stop looking for the thing itself.

Cicero is clear about what makes friendship possible. The chief good must be virtue. Two people who are friends in his sense are not chiefly bound by shared interests, shared schooling, shared ambition. They are bound by a shared sense of what is actually worth wanting. If your shared good is the next promotion, your friendship will not survive the day one of you gets it and the other does not. If your shared good is the firm's billing target, your friendship will not survive the slow year. The friendships that survive are the ones rooted in something that does not shift when fortune shifts — a common conviction about justice, a common debt to a particular faith, a common refusal to do something cheap. Cicero says this is rare. He is right. Most people you meet at the bar are not candidates for this. A few are. Pay attention to which few.

Notice the order in his definition. Goodwill and affection come after the consensio in things divine and human. Cicero does not deny that affection matters; he refuses to put it first. Modern friendship inverts the order — we feel an affinity and call it friendship and then are surprised when the affinity does not survive a hard disagreement. Cicero's old Roman wisdom holds the disagreement does not break the friendship; the agreement on the underlying things is what makes it possible to disagree without breaking. You can argue with such a friend about a Supreme Court case for an hour, take opposite sides at a moot, file briefs against each other in a real one, and walk out to dinner unchanged, because the argument was inside a wider accord.

There is a particular kind of legal friendship that approaches this. You know the man because you tried a case against him five years ago. He impressed you by being honest in a moment he could have hidden something. You impressed him similarly. Slowly, over years, in chance encounters at the bar association and at depositions in other matters, the relation deepened. You discovered you shared a faith, or near enough. You discovered you both read history. You discovered you both, in your private hours, despised the same cheap moves in the profession. The friendship is real and you would not have predicted it from the first introduction. It is, in fact, exactly the kind of friendship Cicero describes — an accord in the things that matter, slow to form, durable when formed.

The Ecclesiasticus passage you read this morning was written some centuries before Cicero, in a different tongue, in a different city, and it says the same thing more compactly. A faithful friend is a strong defence: he that hath found him, hath found a treasure. The Wisdom tradition and the Roman tradition meet on this point, and the Church, when she received both, did not hesitate to combine them. Aquinas will read Cicero on friendship and then say that the highest friendship is charity itself, friendship between God and man. That is tomorrow. Today, sit with the foundation. Friendship is rare. It is not the same as the network you have built. It is given, when it is given, by God, and it is held in place by a shared sense of what is divine and what is human. Without that agreement, you have something else, and the something else is fine, but you should not lean on it as if it were friendship.

If you can name one such friend tonight, name him. Pray for him before you sleep. Tomorrow, write to him. The accord that holds you together is not visible on the firm's org chart, but it is, in Cicero's sober Roman judgment, the second-best thing the immortal gods have given to man. The first is wisdom, and you may notice that the friend in question is generally also the one with whom you most readily share that.`,
