    meditation: `The brief is due at midnight. It is 10:43 PM. You have been at this all day, and most of the previous day, and the argument is not working — you can feel it not working — and you cannot tell whether you are too tired to see it clearly or whether the structure is actually wrong. Your associate left at nine. Your spouse is asleep. The client expects to read this in the morning and to like it.

You pick up the phone. There is one number you would dial at 10:43 PM, and only one. You dial it.

He answers on the third ring. You can hear that he has put his book down. You apologize for the hour. He waves it off with the same phrase he has used for fifteen years — don't be ridiculous — and asks what is going on. You read him the heading of section III. He says, hmm. You read him the first paragraph. He says, that's fine. You read him the third paragraph and he stops you. Wait, he says. Why are you arguing it that way? You are arguing this on equitable grounds when the real argument is jurisdictional. You will lose if you make this look like a fairness case. The bench is going to think you don't have the law. Move section III before section II. Cut paragraph two entirely. You'll have the brief done by midnight.

He is right. You knew he was going to be right. That is why you called him.

This is what Ecclesiastes is talking about. It is better therefore that two should be together, than one. If one fall he shall be supported by the other. The Preacher is not being sentimental; he is being practical, in the unsentimental Hebrew way. The man alone in the field falls and there is no one to help him up. The man alone in a brief at 10:43 PM cannot see his own argument because he has lived inside it too long. He needs a second pair of eyes — not a colleague who will flatter him, not a researcher who will check his citations, but a friend whose judgment he trusts more than he trusts his own at that hour.

There is a name for that person in your profession. He is called co-counsel, even when he is not formally on the case. He is the lawyer you would call at 11 PM and would not feel guilty about the hour. The relation is built up over years, mostly outside any particular file. You worked together once on a small matter. You both showed up. You both did what you said you would do. He took the messier piece without complaining. You took the messier piece next time. Slowly, you became the person each other could call.

Aristotle, in Book VIII of the Nicomachean Ethics, says that friendship is necessary not chiefly for prosperity but for adversity. The friend is the refuge in misfortune. You know this from your own practice. The friend at the bar is not the man you celebrate the win with — though you do, and the celebration is sweeter for being shared. The friend at the bar is the man you call when the wheels are coming off, when the witness collapsed on cross, when the client is suing you, when you are not sure whether the brief due tonight is good or terrible and you cannot tell anymore. He is the refuge.

This is in part why the gospel makes friendship the highest analogy for the soul's relation to God. The dimension of friendship that the Hebrew Preacher and the Greek philosopher both notice — the friend is for adversity — is the dimension on which our most fundamental need rests. We do not, finally, need a God who is good to us in our prosperity, although we have one. We need a God we can call at 10:43 PM on the worst day. The fact that Christ in John 15 calls his disciples friends, the night before he is arrested and they are scattered, is significant. He is not naming a fair-weather relation. He is naming a relation strong enough to hold what is about to happen.

A young lawyer reading this should pay particular attention. The relations you are forming in your first decade at the bar will be the ones you call at 11 PM in your fifth decade. Build them slowly and well. The man you cut corners with in your second year will be the man who cuts corners with you in your tenth. The man you took the boring piece for, and who took the boring piece for you, will be the man you can call at any hour for the rest of your working life. These things compound. They compound the wrong way too. Reputation among your peers is not a marketing problem; it is the slow construction, or the slow demolition, of the network of trust that you will live inside for forty years.

A practical word. Be the person who picks up. The friendship is reciprocal or it dies. If you are willing to be called at 11 PM, you must also be willing to take the call at 11 PM. This requires that you not resent the call. The man on the other end is not interrupting your evening; he is honoring you by trusting you with the moment of his greatest professional doubt. Honor him back. Put the book down. Listen. Give him the answer, or tell him you do not know but you will think about it for ten minutes and call back, and then call back.

The phone call ends. You hang up. You move section III before section II, cut paragraph two of the new section III, and the structure comes clear. The brief is done at 11:48 PM. You file it. You will sleep. The client will read it in the morning and find it improved by an argument that was not yours alone — that was, in its quiet way, the joint work of two friends who have been at this together for long enough that one of them could pick up his phone at 10:43 PM and not have to explain anything.

Thank him in the morning. Tell him the brief was better because of him. Friendships, like virtues, atrophy if they are not exercised, and the exercise is mostly small acknowledgments after the fact. The threefold cord is not easily broken, but it is woven by small motions, and you have to keep weaving.`,
