    meditation: `Yesterday's meditation was about the lawyer you call at 11 PM. Today's is harder. It is about the lawyer you used to call at 11 PM, and have stopped calling, because somewhere along the way the relation went bad.

Every working bar has these stories. They are rarely told, because no one comes out of them looking well. You were close. You shared cases. You shared a year of legal practice and several of life. Then something happened. He took the credit for a brief that was mostly your work. He repeated to a partner the thing you said to him at lunch, which he had been told in confidence. He left the firm and took a client you had brought in, and did not tell you he was going to. Or — the more common variant — nothing dramatic happened at all. The relation just slowly went cold, and you found yourself less and less willing to be in the room with him, until one day you realized you had stopped trusting him and could not remember the moment it had happened.

The psalm you read this morning is one of the most painful in the psalter, and it is not about an enemy. The psalmist could have borne an enemy's reviling — he could have hidden from him, as one hides from any other danger. The wound he names is the wound of the man of one mind, my guide, and my familiar, who didst take sweetmeats together with me: in the house of God we walked with consent. The Latin Vulgate calls him familiaris meus, my familiar, the same root word Aquinas uses for friendship with God. The pain of the psalm is precisely that the loss is the loss of a friend, not an enemy. The man who shared the table and the prayer is the man who has now broken the bond.

Proverbs is, characteristically, more clinical. To trust to an unfaithful man in the time of trouble is like a rotten tooth and a weary foot. Both images are physical: the body has a part that should be reliable and is not. You bite down on what should be a sound tooth and pain runs up the jaw. You step forward on what should be a sound foot and it gives. The Hebrew sage is teaching the practical lesson that the spiritual writers will later teach more delicately: an unreliable friend is worse than no friend at all in trouble, because you have leaned on a thing that does not hold.

How does a lawyer come to this? Cicero, in De Amicitia, treats the question gravely. He says friendships go wrong for many reasons but two are most common. The first is when a friend, raised by fortune above his old companions, comes to find them inconvenient. The second is when the agreement on the things that matter — Cicero's consensio in things divine and human — was never as deep as both parties had supposed, and the first real test reveals the shallowness. The relation looked like friendship; it was actually an alliance of convenience; the convenience expired and so did the relation.

You will recognize both patterns. The first is the partner who made partner before you did and stopped returning your calls. The relation was real, but it was not strong enough to survive his being above you. The second is the lawyer who agreed with you on everything until the firm pressured both of you to do the questionable thing, and you said no, and he said yes, and you both discovered that what you had taken for a shared moral sense was actually a shared cultural surface. Cicero is unsentimental about this. Some friendships were never quite what we took them for, and the discovery is sad but instructive. The friendships that survive such tests are the real ones.

Here is the danger of the broken friendship at the bar: not that you will be hurt by it, although you will. The danger is that you will let it harden you into the kind of lawyer who no longer trusts anyone, who treats every collegial relation as a transaction in advance, who keeps his cards close because of the one set of cards that was once turned against him. This is the bitter ex-friend's curse on you, and it works whether he intended it or not. The wound becomes a teacher of withdrawal. After enough such wounds, you are alone in your office, civil to everyone, intimate with no one, telling yourself you have wised up when in fact you have shrunken.

The Christian way through is harder than withdrawal and harder than naive re-trust. It begins with two acts: honesty about what happened, and refusal to let the broken friendship become the template. Honesty: name to yourself what he did and what you did. He may have been the chief wrongdoer; you were rarely an innocent party, and the discipline of confession is for this exact reason. Forgive him. Forgiveness does not require reconciliation, which depends on him as well as you; forgiveness requires only that you let go of the right to be his judge. Pray for him. The medieval practice of praying by name for the one who has injured you is not pious decoration; it is the actual surgery that detaches the wound from your daily life.

Then, the refusal to let the template harden. The next colleague who shows promise of becoming a real friend deserves to be met as himself, not as a re-edition of the man who failed you. The old wound makes this difficult. You will catch yourself doing it — testing him, holding back, expecting the betrayal that has not happened. Notice the pattern. Bring it to confession. Try again. The slow work of trusting another lawyer after you have been burned is one of the hidden penances of a long career, and the rebuilding, when it happens, is itself a small resurrection.

A note for the senior lawyer. Younger lawyers are watching how you treat your peers — how you speak of an opposing counsel in private, how you handle the colleague who once wounded you. They are learning, from your example, whether the bar is the kind of small civilization in which friendship is possible. Conduct yourself as though it is.

Pray tonight for the colleague who broke trust with you. Pray for him by name, for the part you played, and ask the Lord to redeem the loss without requiring you to pretend it did not happen. The familiar of the psalm became, in the long providence, the type of Judas, and the type of Judas became, in another providence, the occasion of the redemption of the world. Your smaller losses, taken to the foot of the cross, may yet bear a smaller fruit. They will not bear it if you keep them in the locked drawer of bitterness instead of bringing them out.`,
