    meditation: `She was your moot court partner. Second year. You wrote the brief together over six weeks in the law library, alternating sections, fighting about the order of arguments, finally agreeing that the privacy argument went first and the federalism argument went second, drafting until 2 AM the night before. You won regionals. You came in second at nationals to a team from a school you both privately despised. You spent the bus ride home rehearsing what you should have said at the bench's third question. You graduated together. You both clerked. You both went to firms in the same city. You were in each other's weddings.

Now, fifteen years later, she is opposing counsel on a major matter. Not a small matter — a major one. There is a $40 million exposure for your client, and her client is the one trying to extract it. The case will go on for two years. There will be twelve depositions. There will be discovery motions every two months. The two of you have not had a real conversation in eight years, because the work has not allowed it, and now the work has made it impossible.

The matter is in nobody's hand. It is not that either of you behaved badly. The partners chose. The clients chose. The conflict-of-interest letter went out from her firm explaining why she would be taking the case. Your firm responded that they would be opposing. The bar has fewer of these stories than novels suggest, but it has more than the bar itself remembers. Two lawyers who were once one — to use Augustine's phrase — are now in different rooms, and the rooms are not changing for two years.

Augustine wrote his most painful correspondence on exactly this dynamic, although the matter at issue was theological rather than litigation. He and Jerome — two of the great minds of the patristic west, men who admired each other across the Mediterranean — fell into a long, slow, partly-bitter exchange about whether Paul had rebuked Peter sincerely or only ostensibly at Antioch. Augustine took one view, Jerome the other. The correspondence ran for years. Both men understood that the issue itself, while important, was being used by something larger to test their friendship. Augustine, in the letter excerpted today, names what he fears: that the providence of God uses our trials to separate us in spirit, even when it does not separate us in place. It is one thing to be far from one's friend; it is another, and worse, to be of a different mind from him, when the very fact of having been one makes the loss double.

Litigation does this to former co-conspirators of moot court. The providence here is not malicious; God is not arranging the conflict to wound either of you. But the conflict is real, and you must decide, on each motion and each deposition and each call between you and her, what kind of opposing counsel you are going to be. The options narrow quickly. You could become brittle and formal — communicating only in writing, refusing the small courtesies, treating every motion as a chance to embarrass. You could become falsely warm — performing the old friendship at depositions, sending Christmas cards, while doing in the file what you would not do in front of anyone you actually loved. Or you could find the third thing, which is harder and which the Christian tradition has more of a vocabulary for than the law schools admit.

The third thing is honest opposition inside continuing regard. You file the motion the case requires. You take the deposition the case requires. You do not pull punches; she would not forgive you if you did. But you do not put into the briefs the snide footnote that you could put in; you do not use the deposition to embarrass her witness beyond what the answer requires; you return her calls within the hour even when the call is going to be hard. And — this is the part that requires actual virtue — you pray for her, by name, before the depositions you take of her witnesses. The prayer is not for her to lose. The prayer is that the matter come out justly, that neither of you do something cheap, that the friendship, weakened but not killed, survive the two years of being on opposite sides.

Abraham and Lot in Genesis 13 is the older version of this. The herdsmen fight; the land is not large enough for both households; Abraham, the elder, declines to insist on his prerogative. Let there be no quarrel. We are brethren. Take the land you choose; I will take the other. They part — but they part as brothers, and a few chapters later when Lot is captured Abraham raises an army to rescue him. The separation did not have to be enmity. It could be sober and provisional. The case lasted as long as it lasted, and then they were brothers again.

The conflict-of-interest letter is the Genesis 13 of your generation. It is the formal acknowledgement that two lawyers, who would otherwise sit at the same dinner, cannot for a season sit on the same side. The right response to it is grief, not bitterness. Grief is the appropriate human emotion when something good is interrupted by something necessary. Bitterness is what grief becomes when you refuse to call it grief.

Practical counsel. First, do not stop praying for her because you are now opposing her. The temptation is to drop her from the list precisely when she most needs to be on it. Second, do not use the litigation to indulge feelings you have been carrying about her from the years before. The conflict deserves a clean fight, not a proxy one. Third, find the small courtesies that the rules allow and the case does not require — the extension on a deadline that does not matter, the courtesy copy emailed without being asked, the brief acknowledgment when she has filed something well. None of this is weakness; it is the maintenance of the friendship through a long professional winter. Fourth, when the case ends — settlement, verdict, appeal exhausted — call her. Suggest dinner. Do not pretend the two years did not happen. Let them have been what they were. Move on if she will, and grieve if she will not.

The friendship that survives the case will be tougher than the one that did not have to. The friendship that does not survive will have done its work, in its time, and the memory of it is itself a participation in the friendship of God, who lost human friends often and went on loving them. Sleep with that tonight if you can.`,
    prayer: `Lord, you know the lawyer on the other side of my hardest current matter, and you know what she was to me before the work made us opponents. Keep both of us honest in the file. Keep me from the cheap blow, and from the false warmth that would betray the honesty of opposition. Save the friendship for the day after the case ends. Amen.`,
