    meditation: `The sentence is short and the Latin is shorter: iam non dicam vos servos. I will not now call you servants. The text is from the long discourse at the Last Supper, the night before Christ is arrested. He has just washed their feet. He has just identified the betrayer. He is twenty-four hours from the cross. He says this to them in that hour.

Pause on the situation. The disciples are not in a heroic moment. They are confused, sleepy, frightened. Most of them will run before sunrise. Peter will deny him three times before the cock crows. Judas has already left the room. And it is to this group — not a successful group, not an impressive group, a group about to fail him at every level — that he says: I will not now call you servants. The change of name is not earned. It is given.

If you are tempted to think you must achieve friendship with God by your own performance, the timing of John 15 is meant to break that thought. Christ does not say, after Pentecost you will be friends. He does not say, when you have proven yourselves in the great trial that begins tomorrow, then I will call you friends. He says it now, before the failures. He says it precisely now, before. The friendship is constituted by his word, not by the disciples' character. Their character will catch up later, but it does not catch up first. The friendship comes first.

This is a hard word for a lawyer to receive. You make your living by performance. You bill in increments; you are measured by deliverables; your name is on briefs the quality of which determines your professional standing. You inhabit a profession that has, almost by structure, instilled in you the conviction that approval is earned in real time. The category of being given a relation prior to performance is foreign to your daily grammar. It is the gospel's first move on you, and the move it has to make over and over because you keep forgetting it.

Note the content of the friendship Christ describes. The mark of his calling them friends is that he has confided in them. All things whatsoever I have heard of my Father, I have made known to you. Friendship, for him, is the shared confidence — the same Aristotelian and Ciceronian element you read about earlier in the week, the communicatio of life. The Father's life has been opened to the disciples through the Son's confidence in them. They are friends because they know the Father's business. They are not servants because servants do not know it.

Newman, in the Parochial and Plain Sermons, traces the implication. If Christ is the friend, then every human friendship is, when truly examined, an image and foretaste of his. The friendships you have at the bar, the friendship you have with your spouse, the friendship you formed with your moot court partner in the law library at 2 AM — these are not separate from your friendship with God. They are particular instances of the universal friendship that the Incarnation has established between God and the human race. The love between brother and brother is a ray of the love that streamed from the cross. Newman is not being lyrical; he is being theologically literal. The cross is the founding act of Christian friendship, and every smaller friendship is downstream of it.

This means several things for a Christian lawyer.

First, your friendships at the bar have a depth they would not have without Christ. The man you call at 11 PM is not just a useful colleague; he is, in the order of grace, a near-sacramental presence — a small image of the High Priest who is interceding for you above. Every time the friendship at the bar holds firm in adversity, you are seeing the action of the friendship of Christ working through human matter. Take the friendships more seriously. They are theologically more important than your professional vocabulary admits.

Second, the loneliness many lawyers experience is partly the loss of this dimension. The lawyer who has stopped practicing his faith but kept his bar friendships often notices, around year fifteen or twenty, that the friendships have flattened without his being able to say why. The flattening is real. The friendships have lost the upward reference that gave them their depth. Cicero noticed this without being able to name it; he said friendship requires consensio in things divine and human, and when the divine drops out the consensio thins. The remedy is not to recover Cicero. The remedy is to recover the Friend who said, on the night before he died, I will not now call you servants. He has not stopped saying it. Your bar friendships, returned to that ground, gain back the dimension they have lost.

Third, the obligation Christ lays on the disciples in the same paragraph is the strange one: that you love one another. The you is plural. The friendship he has constituted between himself and them obligates them to a friendship among themselves. The vertical relation requires the horizontal one. You cannot, in the order of John 15, be a friend of Christ and treat the colleague at the next desk as merely a useful associate. The friendship of Christ obligates you to extend the same posture, in your small measure, to the people he has put around you. Newman: to know him is to know what friendship really is, for the first time, and to be enabled, by knowing it, to be the friend that, before, we could only attempt to be.

A practical exercise tonight. Take the prayer of intercession you say before sleep — you may already have one, or you may not — and place into it, by name, the five colleagues at the bar who are closest to you. Not as professional contacts. As friends. Pray for them the way Christ in John 17 prays for his disciples: keep them, sanctify them, may they be one. The prayer will feel slightly strange the first time you pray it that way, because you have not used that vocabulary for them before. The strangeness is itself a sign that something needs to be adjusted. The Lord has called you friend; the people he has put next to you are also called friend; the friendship is meant to be at the table together.

The Latin Mass tradition saves the most intimate moments of friendship for the few seconds before communion. The priest says, in the older form, Domine, non sum dignus — Lord, I am not worthy. He goes on to say that the Lord should say only the word and his soul will be healed. The exchange is the exchange of friends, and the Bread is the body of the Friend, given to make the friendship more than ceremonial. Each Mass is the renewal of John 15. Each communion is the audible repetition, sub specie sacramenti, of the same sentence: I will not now call you servants. I have called you friends. Go and love one another, because I have loved you.`,
