    meditation: `The adversary system rests on a paradox the public never quite believes. Two lawyers oppose each other ferociously in court and then walk out together to the cafeteria. They cross-examine a witness who has just contradicted their case under oath, and then exchange a joke at the elevator about the judge's tie. The public reads this as proof that lawyers are cynics; that the courtroom is theater; that what they say to each other in pleadings is not what they think. The reading is wrong. The reality is more interesting and more important. The adversary system can function only because the bar is, beneath the cases, a fraternity. Without the fraternity, the system would collapse into the war of all against all that the cases by themselves would produce.

This is not a sentimental claim. It is a structural one. Every working bar has unwritten rules — that you do not embarrass opposing counsel in court without cause, that you grant the reasonable extension, that you do not bait the witness with traps you would resent if used against your own, that you accept the other side's word in small matters, that you do not, when the case ends, carry the case into the rest of your life. These rules are not in the Rules of Professional Conduct, though some of them have echoes there. They are in the air the courthouse breathes. They are taught by the older lawyers to the younger ones by example more than by speech. They are what makes it possible for the system to be adversarial without being savage.

The Christian tradition has a name for the deeper version of this. Christ in Matthew 5 commands the disciples to love their enemies. He does not soften the command. He does not turn the enemies into friends in disguise. He acknowledges, in the very wording, that they are enemies — that there is real opposition, real harm done. Then he commands the love anyway. The reason given is not utilitarian. The reason is that the Father makes his sun to rise upon the good and the bad, and raineth upon the just and the unjust. The Christian is to behave like the Father — to extend, beyond the boundary of mutual benefit, a goodwill that crosses lines of allegiance.

Translate that to the bar. Opposing counsel is not your enemy in any biblical sense; he is your opponent in a specific matter, a much smaller category. But the command scales down. You are to behave toward the opponent the way the Father behaves toward those who do not love him: with a basic goodwill that does not depend on him deserving it, and that does not stop when the case is going badly for you. The cross-examination remains. The motion to compel remains. The closing argument remains. What is forbidden is the small cruelty, the unnecessary humiliation, the line in the brief that exists only to wound.

Newman, in the famous passage from the Idea of a University, describes the gentleman: one who never inflicts pain. The whole passage is worth memorizing, though it is too long to print here in full. He is tender towards the bashful, gentle towards the distant, and merciful towards the absurd. He has no ears for slander or gossip, is scrupulous in imputing motives to those who interfere with him, and interprets every thing for the best. Newman is describing, in nineteenth-century English, almost exactly what the older bar means when it calls a lawyer a gentleman — a man with whom one can litigate, hard, and shake hands afterward without insincerity. Newman knew the type because the Inns of Court, in his day, were full of it.

The type is endangered. The pressures of modern practice — the social media instantiation of every dispute, the firm websites that crow about scorched-earth tactics, the cable-news habit of contempt — work against the type. Every working bar has a few partners who specialize in being unpleasant, and the unpleasantness is presented as a service. It is not a service. It is a corrosion of the only fabric that holds the system together. The lawyer who treats every opposing counsel as a domestic enemy is not a fierce advocate; he is a free-rider on the civility of everyone else.

This week has been about friendship — Cicero, Aquinas, the eleven-o'clock friend, the colleague who broke trust, the former co-counsel who is now opposing counsel. The week ends here, with the question of what friendship can mean across the bar. The Christian answer is twofold. First, friendship in the full sense, of the kind Aquinas describes — accord in the things divine and human — is rare across the bar, as it is rare everywhere, and it is not the standard you can impose on a relationship structured by opposition. You will not be friends in that full sense with most of the lawyers you face. Second, the analogue of friendship that the bar at its best embodies — fraternity, courtesy, mutual recognition of dignity, the practice of opposing without despising — is achievable across the bar, is in fact required of you by Christ in Matthew 5, and is one of the small ways the Kingdom is built inside an adversary system.

Practical disciplines. First, before a hard hearing or deposition, take three minutes to pray for opposing counsel by name — not for him to lose, but for him to be honest, to do his job, to go home to his family. The three minutes change you, not him. Second, honor the older bar's small courtesies. Grant the extension when it does not hurt your client. Return calls promptly. Do not file the motion if a phone call will solve it. Third, when you are tempted to write the cutting footnote, do not. It will not help your client. It will only mark you, in the file and in your own soul, as the kind of lawyer who writes such footnotes.

Fourth, the longest discipline: build the bar you want to belong to. Show up at the bar association. Take on the unglamorous committee work. Mentor the younger lawyer who reminds you of yourself fifteen years ago. The bar is a small civilization, sustained by the people who sustain it.

The week closes with one image. The Latin Mass at the kiss of peace — pax tecum — has the priests exchanging the small embrace of fellow servants of the same Lord: brothers at the same altar, before the same sacrifice, exchanging the physical sign that they are not at war with each other. The bar at its best practices a secular version of the same gesture — the handshake at the end of the hearing, the nod across the courtroom, the courtesy in the elevator. Practice it. It is not weakness. It is the small daily liturgy by which the system you have been given the privilege to serve continues to be possible.

A faithful friend, Ecclesiasticus says, is a strong defence. So is a faithful adversary, which is a different thing and a related one. May you, by the end of your career, have a long list of both.`,
